<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ikiza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as 1972 Burundian genocide</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ikiza ('the Catastrophe') was a campaign of mass killings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> in 1972, in which the Tutsi-dominated army and the ruling party's youth wing killed perhaps 100,000 to 300,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>. It followed a Hutu rebellion in the south in April 1972 that had killed thousands of Tutsi. Educated Hutu were systematically targeted, and many scholars describe the killings as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ikiza.jpg" alt="Ikiza" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, United States Central Intelligence Agency · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CIA_map_of_Burundi_and_surrounding_countries_during_1972_killings.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> had three main indigenous groups, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>, Tutsi and Twa. Under German rule from the late 1800s and then as part of Ruanda-Urundi, a mandate and later trust territory administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the old social order was left intact: the Tutsi minority kept its high status as an aristocracy and the Hutus stood at the bottom. On 30 March 1972 the former king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntare-v">Ntare V</a> flew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> from exile in Uganda and was at once placed under house arrest. Why he returned is still disputed; some accounts say he had been promised safety by President Micombero, others that Uganda's President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> handed him over.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Between 20:00 and 21:00 on 29 April, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> militants launched attacks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a> and in the southern provinces of Rumonge, Nyanza-Lac and Bururi. Operating in bands of 10 to 30 with automatic weapons, machetes and spears, and joined by Zairean exiles called Mulelists, they killed Tutsis, burned homes and slaughtered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>.</p><p>Once the rising was crushed, the government turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> elite. All former Hutu ministers were detained in the first week, among them the four who had sat in the cabinet that morning, and were quickly killed. The United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees estimated that 131 Hutu officers had been killed by late May. According to the American ambassador, Thomas Melady, about 500 Hutu soldiers and some 2,000 civil servants in the capital were also detained. No public trials were held, and victims from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a> were buried in a mass grave at Buterere. About 75 percent of educated Hutus were killed. Micombero claimed 100,000 dead shared equally between Hutus and Tutsis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The killings set off a large exodus, mostly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutus</a>. About 35,000 had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> by mid-1973, and Tanzania, which took most of them, held an estimated 80,000 by the end of 1974, many settled at Ulyankulu in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabora">Tabora</a> region. In the camps a harsher Hutu ethnic awareness took shape; radical refugees founded the Parti pour la libération du peuple Hutu, which organised attacks on Tutsis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> in 1988.</p><p>The UN Commission on Human Rights dismissed a complaint against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> in 1975, but in 1985 the UN retroactively labelled the killings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Scholars still differ over whether to call them genocide, selective genocide or massacre. No one has ever been charged. Little was known beyond refugees' and missionaries' accounts until Jean-Pierre Chrétien and Dupaquier published Burundi 1972, au bord des génocides in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ikiza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a Hutu rebellion in 1972 the army killed perhaps 100,000 to 200,000 Hutu, mainly the educated, in the killings known as the <b>Ikiza</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-burundi">Republic of Burundi</a></li></ul></section>
